<Doc title="Docs" description="KILN documentation: the agent workflow, the full notation reference and the changelog.">
  <h1>KILN docs</h1>
  <p>
    These pages are generated from the files that ship with KILN, so they always match the release. The same
    text is what your agent reads: the skill installs it into your repo, and the MCP server serves it as
    <code>kiln://workflow</code> and <code>kiln://reference</code>.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={asset("docs/workflow/")}>Workflow</a>: how an agent sets up a game's 3D art, picks or makes a style, makes, rigs and animates a model, and builds it.</li>
    <li><a href={asset("docs/reference/")}>Notation reference</a>: shapes, parts, patterns, decals, rigs, animations, styles, <code>kiln.toml</code>, commands, lint rules, build output.</li>
    <li><a href={asset("docs/changelog/")}>Changelog</a>: every release.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Five-minute start</h2>
  <pre><code>pip install "kiln @ git+https://github.com/lukasgolino/KILN"
kiln init my-art --style soft_toy
cd my-art
kiln new model pilot --template bean
kiln lint && kiln preview pilot   # previews/pilot/turntable.png
kiln build                        # build/models/pilot.glb + manifest.json</code></pre>
  <h2>Sister libraries</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="https://pagan.top/">PAGAN</a> does the same for pixel art (sprites, tiles, icons, UI) and
    <a href="https://segan.top/">SEGAN</a> for game audio. A 3D game usually wants all three.
  </p>
</Doc>
